//BUG="FEEDBACK.md 1.10 — postfix operators do not apply to a call result"
//OUT="0"

// parse_value returns immediately when it recognises a function call
// (src/compiler/parser/expression.rs), before the loop that handles `.field`,
// `[i]` and `: T`. So `Str().size()` is "Unexpected token", while the same
// thing through a variable is fine:
//
//     s = Str()
//     x = s.size()        // OK
//
// For a language whose goal is to be as short as possible, having to name
// every intermediate value is a real cost — `read_str().size()` is exactly
// what people will write.
//
// Fix: fall through into the postfix loop with the call as `res` instead of
// returning early.

fn main
    x = Str().size()
    out "{x}"
